SYNTHESIS
==============================================================================
1. The section-node idea is exact at q=n in open cells: LRC is
   emptiness of the two danger sections adjacent to the observer.
2. Pure class quotients appear when occupancy is remembered as a color
   on danger sections.  Without that color, pressure and void maps can
   compress aggressively but may mix safe and unsafe states.
3. Boundary-flux tournaments are dynamic: their edges change exactly when
   runners enter or leave fixed section boundaries.  They are less pure
   alone, but they look like a useful clock derivative for BLEX/near-graph.
4. q=2n half-sections pay a label tax but separate left/right boundary
   events.  This is a concrete bridge from section nodes to sentinel maps.
5. Future agents should challenge the default vertex choice before coding:
   runners, sections, boundaries, gaps, events, covers, residues, and proof
   obligations are all possible tournament vertex sets.
